VERDICT
Correct: your code successfully counts the spaces in each sentence to figure out the number of words, and correctly tracks the highest score.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You used a very smart math trick to count words by just counting spaces. You also kept the memory usage as small as possible by not creating any new strings or arrays.

Is it correct? Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no mistakes based on the problem rules. The problem guarantees sentences only have single spaces between words and no extra spaces on the ends. If it had extra spaces, your code would count too many words.

Time and space complexity of MY code: Time complexity is O(N) where N is the total number of characters across all sentences, because you look at every letter once. Space complexity is O(1) extra space because you only use two number counters.

Why my approach works: If you have a sentence like "hello world there", there are two spaces. Two spaces separate exactly three words. Adding 1 to the space count always gives the exact word count.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Nested Loops Counting Spaces (Your Approach)
Idea: Go through each sentence one by one. Check every single letter. If the letter is a space, add one to your space counter. The number of words is the space counter plus one. Keep track of the biggest number of words you find.

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int mostWordsFound(vector<string>& sentences) {
        int max_word = 0;
        for(int i=0; i<sentences.size(); i++){
            int count = 0;
            for(int j=0; j<sentences[i].length(); j++){
                if(sentences[i][j] == ' '){
                    count++;
                }
            }
            if(count+1 > max_word){
                max_word = count+1;
            }
        }
        return max_word;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(N) where N is the total number of characters in all sentences.
Space complexity: O(1) extra space.
What makes the next approach better: We can replace your entire inside loop with a single built-in C++ function, making the code much easier to read and less prone to typing errors.

Approach 2: Using the C++ Count Function
Idea: The C++ standard library has a tool called count. You give it the start of a sentence, the end of a sentence, and the character you want to find (a space). It counts them for you instantly. Then we use the max function to update our highest score.

C++
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int mostWordsFound(vector<string>& sentences) {
        int max_word = 0;
        for (const string& sentence : sentences) {
            int spaces = count(sentence.begin(), sentence.end(), ' ');
            max_word = max(max_word, spaces + 1);
        }
        return max_word;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(N) where N is the total number of characters in all sentences, because the count tool still has to look at every character behind the scenes.
Space complexity: O(1) extra space.
What makes the next approach better: This is the most optimal approach for the problem's strict rules. But if the rules were relaxed, counting spaces would break, so we need a more flexible approach just in case.

Approach 3: String Stream (Splitting into Words)
Idea: A string stream pretends your string is a file. It automatically reads text word by word, skipping over spaces automatically. We just keep pulling out words until the stream is empty, counting each one.

C++
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int mostWordsFound(vector<string>& sentences) {
        int max_word = 0;
        for (const string& sentence : sentences) {
            stringstream ss(sentence);
            string word;
            int current_words = 0;
            while (ss >> word) {
                current_words++;
            }
            max_word = max(max_word, current_words);
        }
        return max_word;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(N) where N is the total number of characters.
Space complexity: O(M) extra space where M is the length of the longest sentence, because the string stream makes copies of the words to read them.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use? You used Approach 1, manually checking every character.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? They expect Approach 2. It shows you know the math trick of counting spaces, but it also shows you know how to use standard C++ tools to keep your code clean and modern.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them: Question: What if there are double spaces or spaces at the very beginning of the sentence? Answer: Counting spaces will break. We would need to use Approach 3 (String Stream) because it automatically skips weird spaces, or write a custom loop that checks if the current character is a letter while the previous character was a space.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence: If words are separated by exactly one space, the number of words is always the number of spaces plus one.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time: You did not make any mistakes, but next time try to use range-based for loops (like for string sentence in sentences) instead of index loops (i and j) to make your code look much cleaner.

The pattern or idea this question is really about: Array traversal and character counting.

